class Solution:
    def longestSpecialPath(self, edges: List[List[int]], nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:


=== MODEL ===
Approach: DFS from the root while maintaining the current root-to-node path. For each value, track the depth (prefix distance) of its most recent occurrence on the path, and maintain a sliding left boundary `lo` (the minimum allowed start index in the path stack) so that all values in the window are unique. For node at depth d with prefix distance D[d], the candidate path starts at some ancestor index s ≥ lo, with length D[d] − D[s−1]; the best is the smallest valid start (to maximize length), and among equal lengths we minimize node count (d − s + 1).

I'll implement it iteratively to avoid recursion limits, restoring the boundary state on backtrack.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-7113", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def longestSpecialPath(self, edges: List[List[int]], nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        n = len(nums)\n        adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n        for u, v, w in edges:\n            adj[u].append((v, w))\n            adj[v].append((u, w))\n\n        # Path stack: pd[i] = distance from root to path node at index i; pd_prev via index.\n        # last[val] = index on current path of the latest occurrence of val (or -1).\n        last = {}\n        pref = []        # pref[i] = distance from root to path node at depth i\n        best_len = 0\n        best_nodes = 1  # used only when best_len is updated; initialized below\n        best_nodes = float('inf')\n        # lo: smallest path index allowed as start of a special path (window left bound)\n        # Stack of frames for iterative DFS: (node, parent, state, saved_lo, saved_last_val, saved_last_prev)\n        # We use an explicit stack.\n        # Initialize root.\n        stack = [(0, -1, 0, None)]\n        depth_of = [0] * n\n        dist_of = [0] * n\n        lo_stack = []  # not used directly; we store lo inside frame\n\n        # Frame: (node, parent, iterator_index, lo_at_entry, prev_last_for_val, val)\n        # Use recursion-like simulation.\n        frames = []\n        def enter(node, parent, d, dist):\n            val = nums[node]\n            prev_occ = last.get(val, -1)\n            lo_entry = lo_holder[0]\n            if prev_occ >= lo_holder[0]:\n                lo_holder[0] = prev_occ + 1\n            last[val] = d\n            pref.append(dist)\n            depth_of[node] = d\n            dist_of[node] = dist\n            # compute best path ending at this node\n            nonlocal best_len, best_nodes\n            start = lo_holder[0]\n            length = dist - pref[start]\n            nodes = d - start + 1\n            if length > best_len or (length == best_len and nodes < best_nodes):\n                best_len = length\n                best_nodes = nodes\n            frames.append([node, parent, 0, lo_entry, prev_occ, val, d])\n\n        lo_holder = [0]\n        enter(0, -1, 0, 0)\n        while frames:\n            fr = frames[-1]\n            node, parent = fr[0], fr[1]\n            if fr[2] < len(adj[node]):\n                nxt, w = adj[node][fr[2]]\n                fr[2] += 1\n                if nxt == parent:\n                    continue\n                d = fr[6] + 1\n                enter(nxt, node, d, pref[-1] + w)\n            else:\n                # exit\n                frames.pop()\n                pref.pop()\n                val = fr[5]\n                prev_occ = fr[4]\n                if prev_occ == -1:\n                    del last[val]\n                else:\n                    last[val] = prev_occ\n                lo_holder[0] = fr[3]\n\n        if best_len == 0 and best_nodes == float('inf'):\n            return [0, 1]\n        return [best_len, best_nodes]\n"}

