class Solution:
    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
I'll use a DP over the state (alternating sum, parity of the next index, product), where the product is capped at limit. Since nums[i] ≤ 12 and limit ≤ 5000, the product set reachable is small. Zeros make the product 0, which I handle explicitly. Tracking product values as a set per (sum, parity) keeps the state space manageable.

The alternating sum ranges within [-n·12, n·12] = [-1800, 1800], so I bound the sum dimension accordingly and skip states out of range.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-5583", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:\n        # State: (alt_sum, parity) -> set of achievable products <= limit.\n        # parity 0: next element is added (even position), parity 1: next is subtracted.\n        # Track a set of nonempty subsequences processed so far.\n        OFF = 12 * len(nums)\n        if abs(k) > OFF:\n            return -1\n        # states: dict keyed by (sum, parity) -> set of products (products <= limit)\n        states = {}\n        best = -1\n        for x in nums:\n            new_states = {}\n            # Start a new subsequence with x at position 0 (added).\n            items = [((x, 1), x)]  # (new_key, product)\n            for (s, p), prods in states.items():\n                # Extend with x: sign depends on parity of current length.\n                if p == 0:\n                    ns, np_ = s + x, 1\n                else:\n                    ns, np_ = s - x, 0\n                for pr in prods:\n                    items.append(((ns, np_), pr * x))\n            for key, pr in items:\n                if pr > limit:\n                    continue\n                if key[0] == k:\n                    if pr > best:\n                        best = pr\n                new_states.setdefault(key, set()).add(pr)\n            # Merge new_states into states (keep old subsequences too).\n            for key, prods in new_states.items():\n                states.setdefault(key, set()).update(prods)\n        return best\n"}

